@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the LAK 2011 reviews of flop 1, Joe's outtakes analysis of flop 1, and the Wikimedia IEG 2013 comments on flop 5. Prompted by a writing-coherence check of flop 1 (review-01-writing-coherence-sketch.md), which showed that prose patterns explain why reviewers could not follow it but not why they would not want it. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@summary Patterns for whether an argument gives its audience reason to
accept it: the evidence its venue counts, a term that earns its place, a
worked case, a route back to the audience, an example of the deliverable,
a plan at a checkable grain, and claims stated as hypotheses. This family
sits between writing-coherence (can the reader follow it?) and the
institutions families (does the arrangement benefit those it asks?).

! conclusion:
  An argument can be clear and still give its audience no reason to
  accept it. Argument coherence is the fit between what the argument
  offers (evidence, cases, deliverables, claims) and what its audience
  needs in order to act on it.

  + context:
    Applied to flop 1, writing-coherence caught the unclear thesis, the
    roadmap opening and the register mismatch, and these matched the
    reviewers. It did not catch "it is rather silly to target an
    analytics conference without concrete data", nor the Wikimedia
    reviewers' question of how the work would carry back to them. Those
    are failures of the argument, not of the prose.

  + IF:
    A paper, proposal or ticket asks an audience to accept, fund or act
    on something.

  + HOWEVER:
    Seven patterns mined from two recorded verdicts are a small base.
    Rejections also have causes outside the argument (fit, timing,
    competition), and hindsight makes flaws easy to find in work known
    to have failed.

  + THEN:
    Review an argument with this family alongside writing-coherence, and
    record rows of (pattern, quoted passage, meets or breaks). Calibrate
    on the two flops with recorded verdicts, test blind on the others,
    and require the controls (Corneli 2016 ICCC; the Peeragogy Handbook)
    to pass.

  + BECAUSE:
    A who-benefits analysis needs a way to say, from the text alone, what
    an argument gives its audience. Patterns mined from real verdicts
    give that, and a flaw found in an archival argument that none of
    them flags becomes a new pattern.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Blind run on flops 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8, plus the two controls.]
    next[Mine further verdicts if rejection notes for flops 2, 3, 4, 6 or 8 turn up.]
